WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.950
The 1992 movie “Candyman” still remains
an influential piece of horror cinema.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:09.790
Now, the highly anticipated sequel in the
franchise is almost here.

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:13.660
For the fans who have been waiting patiently,
here’s everything we know so far.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:17.740
"Candyman" has seen quite a bit of reshuffling
over the last year.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:21.720
Universal originally planned for the horror
sequel to come out in June 2020, but by April

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.520
of that year, the studio pushed it back in
the schedule.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:28.539
The studio set a new, aspirational release
date for September of that year, but it had

00:00:28.539 --> 00:00:33.260
to switch dates several more times before
it landed at its current date of August 27,

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.260
2021.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:38.980
It looks like "Candyman" will stick with that
date, but we can never be too sure with these

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:39.980
things.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:43.960
As far as we know, "Candyman" will come out
exclusively in theaters upon its initial release.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.330
A ton of studios have begun experimenting
with dual theater and streaming service release

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:49.330
strategies.

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:53.100
Still, it looks like Universal wants people
to have the chance to see the newest horror

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.210
flick on the big screen, at least initially.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:00.070
There's no official word yet regarding when
or where the slasher flick will be available

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:04.150
to view at home, but it's likely to follow
the standard progression from theaters to

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:07.240
pay-per-view VOD to subscription streaming.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:11.090
So, don't expect to watch it from your home
until it's concluded the standard theatrical

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:12.090
run.

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:15.020
Some films are delayed for creative reasons.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:19.179
Nothing makes a fanbase nervous like a round
of reshoots, and if they're extensive enough,

00:01:19.179 --> 00:01:21.950
they can definitely inspire a little schedule
shuffling.

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:24.399
Fortunately, that wasn't the case with "Candyman."

00:01:24.399 --> 00:01:29.299
The original June 2020 release was rendered
unworkable by the budding pandemic.

00:01:29.299 --> 00:01:34.479
Universal clearly didn't want to drop a highly-anticipated
tentpole film on a country full of empty theaters.

00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:39.369
So, the first delay came down just as the
pandemic was picking up speed, pushing "Candyman"

00:01:39.369 --> 00:01:42.939
back an overly optimistic five months to September.

00:01:42.939 --> 00:01:46.619
The next delay, sadly, was also a result of
the ongoing pandemic.

00:01:46.619 --> 00:01:51.389
The has pandemic dragged on into 2021, and
continues to drag on in many places around

00:01:51.389 --> 00:01:52.389
the world.

00:01:52.389 --> 00:01:56.979
Theaters around the world were still shuttered
as of 2021, so instead of kicking the film

00:01:56.979 --> 00:02:02.499
to VOD, Universal pushed the release date
again and again, eventually landing on August

00:02:02.499 --> 00:02:03.499
27.

00:02:03.499 --> 00:02:07.889
With the domestic vaccine rollout significantly
stemming the tide of the pandemic, and lockdown

00:02:07.889 --> 00:02:12.110
orders lifting just about everywhere in the
U.S., it now seems almost certain that the

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:13.920
August release date will hold.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.340
Unless something totally unforeseen happens
between now and then, expect "Candyman" to

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:22.209
roll into a theater near you alongside the
dog days of summer.

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:27.430
Fans of the original "Candyman" movies can
expect to see a few familiar faces among the

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:28.430
cast.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:31.400
Most notably, Tony Todd reprises his role
as the title slasher.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:36.290
The Candyman himself is a terrifying spirit
who visits anyone who dares to say his name

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:38.430
five times while looking into a mirror.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:43.030
With a rusty hook as a hand and a propensity
for bees, he's one of the scariest horror

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:45.459
movie monsters to grace the big screen.

00:02:45.459 --> 00:02:49.349
Another original actor to return includes
Vanessa Estelle Williams, who plays Anne-Marie

00:02:49.349 --> 00:02:51.110
McCoy in both movies.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:55.330
You may remember her from the 1992 film as
Anthony's mother, who spoke with a college

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:59.319
student about the urban legend surrounding
the monster within Chicago's Cabrini-Green

00:02:59.319 --> 00:03:00.489
community.

00:03:00.489 --> 00:03:04.970
Anthony is all grown-up for the follow-up,
and Yahya Abdul-Mateen II portrays him this

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:06.060
time around.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:11.099
He's now a visual artist living in the newly-gentrified
area of town, where he explores the myth of

00:03:11.099 --> 00:03:14.180
Candyman to find inspiration for his art.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:18.319
The impressive cast for the upcoming horror
flick also includes such talented actors as

00:03:18.319 --> 00:03:23.150
Teyonah Parris from "WandaVision," Colman
Domingo from "Ma Rainey's Black Bottom," and

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:24.969
Nathan Lloyd Stewart-Jarrett from "Mope."

00:03:25.969 --> 00:03:30.890
Given that 2021's "Candyman" doesn't have
a subtitle or any numbers in the title, it

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:34.640
might be easy for audiences to assume it's
a remake of the original.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:39.349
Rest assured, it's a sequel that picks up
several decades after the first film's events.

00:03:39.349 --> 00:03:43.170
The two movies even share a connection by
making the little boy Candyman kidnapped the

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:45.079
protagonist of this follow-up.

00:03:45.079 --> 00:03:50.319
As for the 2021 film, the plot follows Anthony,
played by Abdul-Mateen II, as he seeks to

00:03:50.319 --> 00:03:52.319
find a way to rejuvenate his art career.

00:03:52.319 --> 00:03:56.299
One day, he meets with an older member of
the community who was once a resident of the

00:03:56.299 --> 00:03:57.920
Cabrini Green projects.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:02.799
This person tells him the haunting story of
Candyman, which Anthony then uses as inspiration

00:04:02.799 --> 00:04:03.799
for his paintings.

00:04:03.799 --> 00:04:08.640
However, the deeper he goes into exploring
the concept of Candyman, the more he'll unleash

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.030
a horrific wave of murders.

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:15.040
The director of the 2021 film, Nia DaCosta,
appeared in a video interview in which she

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:17.669
discussed her approach to "Candyman" in depth.

00:04:17.669 --> 00:04:21.769
The director is clearly a kindred spirit with
her latest collaborator, Jordan Peele.

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:26.890
DaCosta confirms that she shares Peele's firmly
held belief that horror is a perfect vehicle

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:31.100
for the exploration of social issues like
racial and class inequities.

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:35.110
She pointed out in the interview that the
creation of monsters is hardly the sole province

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:36.400
of genre fiction.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:37.699
DaCosta says,

00:04:37.699 --> 00:04:40.940
"We create monsters of men all the time.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:44.750
People are murdered and they become either
saints or they are vilified.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:49.460
So, throughout the last year and a half, it
was always coming back to that truth.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:53.180
Horror is a really effective tool when it
comes to telling stories about things that

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:55.070
impact us on a social level.

00:04:55.070 --> 00:05:00.150
The very function of it is to make you uncomfortable
and I think if that discomfort is attached

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:04.790
to explorations of race or gender you have
to then reconcile your feelings about race

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:05.790
and gender."

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:10.250
Given the vocal sentiments of DaCosta and
horror maestro Jordan Peele, expect a healthy

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:15.319
serving of the latter's signature blend of
frights and social commentary to be on abundant

00:05:15.319 --> 00:05:16.319
display.

00:05:16.319 --> 00:05:21.030
"Candyman" is one horror film that will leave
you thinking twice before you stare too long

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:22.750
at a mirror and say,

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:23.750
"Candyma--"

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:24.750
“Shhh!

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:25.750
Don’t.

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:28.849
Don’t say that.”

00:05:28.849 --> 00:05:32.979
We already know that "Candyman" 2021 has been
pulled forward into the modern day.

00:05:32.979 --> 00:05:37.349
As a result, we can expect the film to grapple
with contemporary issues of anti-Black racism

00:05:37.349 --> 00:05:40.440
in America, using the popular discourse of
the time.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:45.870
Peele has also described this film as a "spiritual
sequel" to the original "Candyman," one that

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:50.370
will likely capture the mood of the original
better than any of its less-lauded direct

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:51.370
sequels.

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:55.819
For a hint at how Peele's new vision might
look, we turn to Universal's extensive synopsis

00:05:55.819 --> 00:05:57.919
of the forthcoming film.

00:05:57.919 --> 00:05:58.919
Universal revealed,

00:05:58.919 --> 00:06:03.540
"For as long as residents can remember, the
housing projects of Chicago's Cabrini-Green

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:08.310
neighborhood were terrorized by a word-of-mouth
ghost story about a supernatural killer with

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:13.400
a hook for a hand, easily summoned by those
daring to repeat his name five times into

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:14.400
a mirror.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:18.639
In present day, a decade after the last of
the Cabrini towers were torn down, visual

00:06:18.639 --> 00:06:23.479
artist Anthony McCoy and his girlfriend, gallery
director Brianna Cartwright, move into a luxury

00:06:23.479 --> 00:06:29.120
loft condo in Cabrini, now gentrified beyond
recognition and inhabited by upwardly mobile

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:30.930
millennials.”

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:32.730
Universal further explained, saying,

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:37.220
“With Anthony's painting career on the brink
of stalling, a chance encounter with a Cabrini

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:43.250
Green old-timer exposes Anthony to the tragically
horrific nature of the true story behind Candyman.

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:47.700
Anxious to maintain his status in the Chicago
art world, Anthony begins to explore these

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:52.729
macabre details in his studio as fresh grist
for paintings, unknowingly opening a door

00:06:52.729 --> 00:06:58.729
to a complex past that unravels his own sanity
and unleashes a terrifying wave of violence

00:06:58.729 --> 00:07:01.569
that puts him on a collision course with destiny.”

00:07:01.569 --> 00:07:06.090
It's worth noting that though the Cabrini-Green
neighborhood still exists, the real-life Cabrini-Green

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:08.760
Rowhouse projects were torn down in 2011.

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:13.949
The Cabrini-Green housing projects were infamous,
and provided an iconic backdrop to the original

00:07:13.949 --> 00:07:15.940
1992 "Candyman."

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:20.560
Just how the new film will grapple with the
changing face of the neighborhood is unknown,

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:25.419
but count on Jordan Peele to find an interesting
way to weave the complex social fabric of

00:07:25.419 --> 00:07:30.370
present-day Chicago into aesthetic or plot
devices to the thesis of this film.

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:35.770
After over a year of delays, there better
be a trailer.

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:39.310
Fortunately, Universal has released not just
one, but two full-length trailers to keep

00:07:39.310 --> 00:07:43.550
viewers excited about the film over the course
of the excruciating wait.

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:49.780
Both trailers, taken in tandem, tease a terrifying
and relevant film marked by immaculate acting,

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:51.930
direction, and cinematography.

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:56.110
The first trailer, released back in February
2020 when that original June release date

00:07:56.110 --> 00:08:00.620
still seemed like a possibility, shows off
quite a bit of the two killer leads, Yahya

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:03.009
Abdul-Mateen II and Teyonah Parris.

00:08:03.009 --> 00:08:07.939
We also get a glimpse of a bunch of teenagers
lined up in what looks like a school bathroom,

00:08:07.939 --> 00:08:11.159
summoning the titular Candyman, while standing
in front of a mirror.

00:08:11.159 --> 00:08:13.410
You know, like you're supposed to do it.

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:17.060
Minor spoiler alert: It doesn't end well for
the teens.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:22.439
The second trailer, which only dropped on
June 23, 2021, takes an even creepier approach.

00:08:22.439 --> 00:08:27.300
It opens with a bit of animated shadow puppetry
to accompany a voiceover explaining the Candyman

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:31.130
mythology and hinting at the danger that will
be summoned in the film.

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:35.839
Interestingly, the plot of the original Candyman
film seems to serve as the lore underpinning

00:08:35.839 --> 00:08:37.479
the contemporary story.

00:08:37.479 --> 00:08:42.630
The voiceover sets up the razor blades-in-the-candy
scenario from the earlier film, as quick intercuts

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:47.080
of the animated shadow puppets continue to
intrude to eery effect.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:51.390
For the fans that want to see how these things
are all pulled together and the ones who just

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:54.440
want a few good scares, “Candyman” is
almost here.

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:57.779
We’ll just have to make our way into the
theatres for the experience.

00:08:57.779 --> 00:09:00.420
“Say his name.”

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:04.720
Looks like we're going to finally find out
what happens when they do in theaters on August

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:06.579
27.

